# PROJECT: COURSE CREATION ECOSYSTEM

Act as Principal Software Architect, Senior Full-Stack Engineer, AI Infrastructure Engineer, UI/UX Architect, and QA Lead.

## Mission
Build a cloud-hosted, modular course-production platform that integrates:
1. Course planning, instructional content, assessments and references.
2. A Canva-inspired editable presentation and visual-design studio.
3. AI-generated images, graphics, diagrams, charts and tables.
4. An ElevenLabs-inspired voice, narration and audio studio.
5. Video rendering, captions, synchronization and LMS exports.
6. A unified gateway for self-hosted and external AI models.

## Constraints
- Initial cloud budget: $200 in available Google Cloud credits.
- Prefer open-source, license-compatible components.
- Avoid paid services unless explicitly approved.
- Keep AI providers interchangeable; never hardwire the platform to one provider.
- Design for scalability, security, portability and low operating costs.
- Treat all reference applications as functional research targets, not source code to copy.
- Use REA when its evidence-based investigation capabilities are relevant.
- Never claim an investigation or test succeeded without actual evidence.

## Stage 1 — Discovery ONLY
Do not build the full application yet.

1. Inspect the REA repository, installed skill instructions, available tools and agent configuration.
2. Report what works, what is missing and any compatibility issues.
3. Research suitable open-source editors, media engines, AI models and cloud components.
4. Verify software licenses and realistic deployment requirements.
5. Design the system architecture, database schema, API contracts and job-processing workflow.
6. Estimate Google Cloud costs and establish spending safeguards.
7. Define an MVP, implementation milestones and measurable acceptance tests.
8. Identify technical risks, unresolved decisions and fallback options.

## Required deliverables
Create a Technical Discovery Pack containing:
- README and project principles
- Architecture specification
- Technology and license comparison
- Database and API design
- AI provider/model capability matrix
- Cloud deployment and cost plan
- Security and data-management plan
- MVP backlog with acceptance criteria
- Testing strategy and implementation roadmap

Save all deliverables in a dedicated documentation directory. Cite sources and distinguish verified facts from estimates.

Stop after discovery. Present the findings and proposed architecture for approval before starting implementation. Preserve existing files and configurations; do not overwrite or install anything without reviewing the impact.
